WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Monday,

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.440
the sixteenth of November.

3
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.400
This episode is written and read with AI.

4
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.560
The International Energy Agency says
the oil market has tipped into surplus

5
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.200
for the first time since
the Iran war began,

6
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.520
as Gulf exports recover. Shipping
through the Strait of Hormuz is still

7
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.360
badly constrained, though, and Iran
claims two more vessels hit mines

8
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.880
there over the weekend.

9
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.240
On the markets. Brent settled near
ninety-six dollars a barrel on Friday,

10
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.800
up from about ninety-one
dollars thirty early Thursday.

11
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.240
December West Texas Intermediate
still lost two dollars sixty on the

12
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.520
week, or two point nine percent.

13
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.640
That made it crude's second
straight weekly fall.

14
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.640
Starting with that surplus call.

15
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.520
The agency's monthly report on
Friday said global supply now exceeds

16
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.000
demand, and it expects a
surplus in the fourth quarter.

17
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.920
Middle East exports are
recovering faster than demand,

18
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.267
despite continued attacks on shipping.

19
00:00:56.293 --> 00:00:59.240
The report doesn't say
how big the surplus is.

20
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.240
Iraq did much of the lifting.

21
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.173
Its October crude output reached
four point six million barrels a day,

22
00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:07.333
the highest since twenty nineteen.

23
00:01:07.387 --> 00:01:11.467
That's up about five hundred and forty
thousand barrels a day from September.

24
00:01:11.493 --> 00:01:14.533
Kuwait and Saudi Arabia
also raised output.

25
00:01:14.587 --> 00:01:18.000
Those barrels are getting out on
smaller tankers that leave Hormuz and

26
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.120
then transfer cargoes to very large
crude carriers in the Gulf of Oman.

27
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.280
That keeps exports moving even
while big-ship transits stay thin.

28
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.200
Buyers in Asia are taking them.

29
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.240
Bloomberg reported that Chinese,
Japanese and Thai refiners are buying

30
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.120
more Gulf crude, including cargoes
that must cross the strait.

31
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.040
Freight has come off record highs
and producers are offering discounts.

32
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.680
That account rests on unnamed traders.

33
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.080
If it holds, Asian refiners may
draw less on Atlantic Basin crude.

34
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.120
Prices didn't follow the bearish
balance late in the week.

35
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.160
Brent's Friday rebound came on attacks
on Saudi infrastructure and continued

36
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.600
threats to shipping.

37
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.920
Staying in the strait. Iran says two
ships hit mines there on consecutive

38
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.560
days, and neither strike has
been independently confirmed.

39
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.920
On Friday, the navy of the Islamic
Revolutionary Guard Corps said a liquefied

40
00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.760
natural gas carrier struck a mine
and suffered an explosion while using

41
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.200
what it called an unauthorised route.

42
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.160
On Saturday, Iran's navy said an
oil tanker caught fire after hitting

43
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.040
a mine in the eastern strait.

44
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.280
That came via Iranian state media,
and Reuters could not verify it.

45
00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.840
The reports we've seen today
give no vessel names or owners,

46
00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.640
and no British maritime security reports.

47
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.320
Even with exports recovering,
shipping remains the bottleneck.

48
00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:38.480
Vessel traffic through Hormuz is about
sixty-five percent below pre-conflict

49
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.640
levels, according to gCaptain.

50
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.440
Airlines in Asia are
feeling it most directly.

51
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.360
Industry executives told Reuters
that Asian jet fuel prices are about

52
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.400
seventy-five percent above pre-war levels.

53
00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.000
Carriers whose hedges are expiring
now have to decide whether to lock

54
00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.680
in at those prices.

55
00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.840
Turning to Russia. Ukraine has now
struck nine Russian refineries since

56
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.240
the start of November,
according to Rigzone,

57
00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.160
up from six by the ninth.

58
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.547
Damage and lost refinery
runs haven't been reported.

59
00:03:07.573 --> 00:03:09.880
The pressure goes beyond refineries.

60
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.760
On Sunday, drones struck three
commercial ships near Romania's Sulina

61
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.293
canal, according to
Romania's prime minister,

62
00:03:16.347 --> 00:03:18.240
and fighter jets were scrambled.

63
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.480
Britain, meanwhile, sanctioned eleven
ships tied to Russian energy exports,

64
00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.627
including five gas carriers
and five oil tankers.

65
00:03:25.653 --> 00:03:30.000
It also listed nine companies, among
them Slavneft and Rosneft's Vostok

66
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.240
Oil project.

67
00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.960
None of that has dented
Moscow's income yet.

68
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.760
The International Energy Agency says
Russia's October revenue from crude

69
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.560
and product exports was
the highest since May,

70
00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.853
lifted by war-driven prices.

71
00:03:42.907 --> 00:03:45.600
In Tehran, the outlook runs the other way.

72
00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.120
A senior budget official told the
government to draft next year's budget

73
00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.160
on the assumption that oil
revenue could fall to zero,

74
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.880
OilPrice dot com reported.

75
00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.920
According to the US Treasury, Iran has
loaded no crude since the twenty-fifth

76
00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.013
of August.

77
00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:01.240
Its oil at sea has dried up too.

78
00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.120
Cargoes held outside the US blockade
fell from nearly eighty-nine million

79
00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.640
barrels in mid-July to
zero by mid-October.

80
00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.787
Chinese buyers pay one to
two months after delivery,

81
00:04:11.813 --> 00:04:16.000
which is why the analytics firm Kpler
expects the revenue to stop soon.

82
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.360
Separately, a restricted report
from the International Atomic Energy

83
00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.240
Agency says Iran is still refusing
inspectors access to verify its enriched

84
00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.800
uranium stockpile.

85
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.160
Over to gas. European power prices
topped six hundred euros a megawatt

86
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.000
hour last week during a wind drought,
according to OilPrice dot com.

87
00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.000
That pushes more demand onto gas-fired
generation just as storage is

88
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:38.900
drawing.

89
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.440
Asia is short as well. Pakistan LNG
Limited has reissued a tender for

90
00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.120
two cargoes into Port Qasim,
after rejecting earlier bids,

91
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.680
Business Recorder reported.

92
00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.600
One is for delivery in mid-December,
the other in early January.

93
00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:56.880
Back in the US, Washington extended
the Jones Act waiver by ninety days

94
00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.520
on Friday. It's the third extension
of a waiver first issued in March.

95
00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.840
A White House official
confirmed it to Reuters,

96
00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.200
and a Customs and Border
Protection notice set it out.

97
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.760
Foreign-flagged tankers can keep moving
oil between US ports into February,

98
00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.360
though each voyage still needs
individual approval under a restriction

99
00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:19.760
added in August. For Gulf Coast shippers,
that means more tankers available

100
00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.360
to carry barrels to other US
coasts through the winter.

101
00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.520
A few more things.

102
00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.480
ExxonMobil signed a memorandum of
understanding with Venezuela's state

103
00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.760
oil company, PDVSA. It gives Exxon
a year to evaluate Venezuelan oil

104
00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.560
areas, including its
former Orinoco project,

105
00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.320
according to World Oil.

106
00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:42.560
Baker Hughes says the total US rig
count fell to six hundred and one,

107
00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.680
while oil rigs held at four
hundred and sixty-three.

108
00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.880
And Hapag-Lloyd has resumed
some Red Sea services.

109
00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.240
What to watch next. Daily EU gas
storage readings from Gas Infrastructure

110
00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.960
Europe continue through the
twenty-sixth of November.

111
00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.040
On Wednesday, the EIA publishes its
weekly report for the week to the

112
00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:01.760
thirteenth.

113
00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.760
Further out, Guyana's first Uaru
cargo, Pakistan's first tender cargo,

114
00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.320
the EU ban on long-term Russian LNG
and the expiry of Russia's diesel

115
00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:16.000
export ban all land between
mid-December and the end of January.

116
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.560
That's the brief. Sources for everything
you heard are in the show notes.
